From 4f71a16bb407aca975bdbc250b55318027594e5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 13:57:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] cursor: let an agent's tool loop resume its conversation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An agent's next step arrives about a second after the reply, and the
checkpoint that would let it resume lands in that window. Three things
made it miss:

The pending marker that exists to make a lookup wait for an in-flight
store was announced under one key while the store wrote two. The second
key is the client's own request prefix, and it is the only one a client
that rewrites the assistant turn can match — which is what an agent
front-end does when it reformats tool calls. Announce every key the
store will write.

The boundary probe never waited at all, on the assumption that only the
exact turn-end transcript could be pending. With the request prefix now
announced too, that no longer holds, so the probe notes the longest
boundary whose store is in flight and waits for that one.

The cache was keyed by account alone, but a checkpoint is a resumable
upstream conversation and upstream pins each to the model that opened
it. Agent clients open every conversation with the same system prompt
and first user message, so two models routinely collided: whichever ran
last overwrote the other's checkpoint, and the loser then found one it
could not use and — because a model mismatch also vetoed the boundary
probe — replayed its whole history uncached for the rest of the
conversation. Scope the cache by model, which makes the mismatch
unreachable rather than merely handled.

Together these were why a task's working steps re-sent everything under
a fresh upstream conversation id: on the panel every step of an agent's
work wrote its whole prompt to cache and read none of it, while the
plain turns that opened and closed the task cached normally.
