From 6c9361e1f96a701d5d8cb9c51b1a4cbfaaab1ab4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 13:57:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] cursor: bill a resumed segment against the prefix it
 resumed from
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A segment that pauses for client tools gets no report from Cursor, so it
is billed from an estimate. The estimate counted what this run had sent,
and a resumed run has sent nothing yet — so a step that continued a
checkpointed conversation looked entirely new.

Two things fell out of that. The prefix the checkpoint already carries
upstream, which upstream serves from its own cache, was credited to
nobody; and the growth beyond it was called a cache write, because the
whole prompt looked like growth. On the wire the Anthropic counters are
disjoint, so a write that swallows the growth leaves input_tokens at
exactly zero: an agent's working steps each reported input 0, cache
creation 42.9K and cache read 0, on a prompt that was in fact almost
entirely cached, and every one of those phantom writes was billed at the
creation premium.

Seed the ledger with the prefix the checkpoint covers, and stop
estimating writes at all. Which part of a prompt upstream commits to its
cache is upstream's decision — it writes at the breakpoints the request
marks and leaves the tail past the last one uncached — so the growth
stays plain input, which is both how it is priced and what it is. The
run's closing report carries the true creation figure and reconciles it,
so nothing is lost.

With the learned read/write shape no longer deciding anything, the
machinery that tracked it goes too.
